<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.5.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01809</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.5.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>b022aaac-4746-4e55-80f5-f190de3393e1</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this
      lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>A rocket is launched in the air and its height, in feet, is modeled by the function \(h\).</item>
</list>
<para>Here is a graph representing \(h\).</para>
<para><figure><media alt="A curve on a graph, origin O, with grid. Horizontal axis, time, seconds, scale 0 to 8, by 1’s. Vertical axis, height, feet, scale 0 to 200, by 20’s. A curve passes through the points 0 comma 5, 3 comma 161, and 5 comma 105."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d4c68792388451be9621c700e4f27732ae382c2c"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Select three</emphasis> true statements about the situation.</para>

<list>
  <item> The rocket is launched from a height less than 20 feet above the ground. </item>
  <item> The rocket is launched from about 20 feet above the ground. </item>
  <item> The rocket reaches its maximum height after about 3 seconds. </item>
  <item> The rocket reaches its maximum height after about 160 seconds. </item>
  <item> The maximum height of the rocket is about 160 feet.</item>
</list>

<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The rocket is launched from a height less than 20 feet above the ground. The rocket reaches
  its maximum height after about 3 seconds. The maximum height of the rocket is about 160 feet.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>A baseball travels \(d\) meters \(t\) seconds after being dropped from the top of a building. The distance
    traveled by the baseball can be modeled by the equation \(d=5t^2\).</item>
</list>
<para>Use this table to answer questions a–g. </para>
<table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        \(t\) (seconds)
      </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
        \(d\) (meters)
      </emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        0
      </entry>
      <entry> </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        0.5
      </entry>
      <entry> </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        1
      </entry>
      <entry> </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        1.5
      </entry>
      <entry> </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        2
      </entry>
      <entry> </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> How many meters (\(d\)) did the baseball travel in 0 seconds (\(t\))? </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 0</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> How many meters (\(d\)) did the baseball travel in 0.5 seconds (\(t\))? </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 1.25</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
    <item> How many meters (\(d\)) did the baseball travel in 1 second (\(t\))? </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 5</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
    <item> How many meters (\(d\)) did the baseball travel in 1.5 seconds (\(t\))? </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 11.25</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
    <item> How many meters (\(d\)) did the baseball travel in 2 seconds (\(t\))? </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 20 </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
    <item> Is this graph an accurate representation of the data in the table? 
      
      <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/76e9e08d68737f77cd4e09f02636aec9922513b8"/></media></figure></item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Yes. The coordinates plotted on the graph match the data in the table: \((0,0)\),
  \((0.5,1.25)\), \((1,5)\), \((1.5,11.25)\), \((2,20)\). </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
    <item> Is the baseball traveling at a constant speed? </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>No. The distance traveled is not changing at a constant speed; it is increasing over time.
  For example, in the first half second, it travels 1.25 meters, and in the next half second, it travels 3.75 meters.
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>A small ball is dropped from a 100-foot-tall building. Which equation could represent the ball’s height,
    \(h(t)\), in feet, relative to the ground, as a function of time, \(t\), in seconds? </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(h(t)=100-16t\) </item>
  <item> \(h(t)=100-16t^2\) </item>
  <item> \(h(t)=100+16t^2\) </item>
  <item> \(h(t)=100-16t\) </item>
</list>

<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(h(t)=100-16t^2\)</para></section>
</content>
